.TH V4-TP 1 "October 29, 2026" "unix4-toolchain"
.ad l
.ds AD l
.nh
.cflags 0 .?!
.ds PROG v4-tp
.SH NAME
\*[PROG] \- create, list and extract UNIX Fourth Edition tp archives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B \*[PROG] \-c
[\fB\-vmD\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIarchive\fR] [\fB\-C\fR \fIdir\fR] [\fB\-b\fR \fIboot\fR]
[\fB\-T\fR \fIlist\fR] [\fB\-U\fR \fIuid\fR] [\fB\-G\fR \fIgid\fR] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.br
.B \*[PROG] \-x
[\fB\-vpi\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIarchive\fR] [\fB\-C\fR \fIdir\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
.br
.B \*[PROG] \-t
[\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIarchive\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
.br
.B \*[PROG] \-k
[\fB\-f\fR \fIarchive\fR]
.br
.B \*[PROG] \-B
\fIbootfile\fR \fB\-f\fR \fIarchive\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B \*[PROG]
works on raw images of the tape format of the same name used by the Fourth
Edition of UNIX (1973): a 512-byte boot block, a directory of 64-byte entries,
then the files, each starting on a block boundary.
It does not understand the SIMH ".tap" record wrapper; unwrap such images first
(a warning is printed if one is detected).
.PP
Only regular files are archived, under their relative path names (at most 31
characters). Directories are created when extracting.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Create an archive from the named files; directories are archived recursively in
byte order. With no file arguments and no
.B \-T
the current directory is archived.
.TP
.B \-x
Extract the named entries (or everything). A name selects itself and
everything below it. Modification times are restored; permissions are
restored subject to the umask.
.TP
.B \-t
List names; with
.B \-v
also mode, uid/gid, size, UTC time and first block.
.TP
.B \-k
Verify every directory checksum and that all data lies inside the archive.
.TP
.BI \-B " file"
Write the boot block (block 0) to
.IR file .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " archive"
Archive file; \fB\-\fR means standard input/output.
.TP
.B \-m
Magtape layout: 62 directory blocks, 496 files, data from block 63 (default).
.TP
.B \-D
DECtape layout: 24 directory blocks, 192 files, data from block 25, and the
image is padded to the tape's 578 blocks. When reading, the layout is detected
automatically unless \fB\-m\fR or \fB\-D\fR is given.
.TP
.BI \-C " dir"
Change to
.I dir
first (the target directory for \fB\-x\fR, created if needed).
.TP
.BI \-T " list"
Read file names, one per line, from
.I list
(\fB\-\fR for standard input). The order of the list is the order on the tape.
.TP
.BI \-b " file"
Store the first 512 bytes of
.I file
as the boot block (default: zeros).
.TP
.B \-p
When extracting, also restore set-uid/set-gid bits and, if run as root, the owner.
Without it the archive can never create a set-id file.
.TP
.B \-i
Report damaged directory entries but do not let them affect the exit status.
.TP
.BI \-U " uid" ", " \-G " gid"
Store this uid/gid for every file. Real ids above 255 do not fit the format
and are stored as 0.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose.
.SH SAFETY
Extraction refuses names containing "..", strips a leading "/", never follows
symbolic links in the path and does not create set-id files unless
.B \-p
is given.
.SH FORMAT
See \fBv4-tp(5)\fR and \fBv4-fs(5)\fR.
Times are 32-bit seconds since 1970, high-order word first; sizes are 24 bits;
the 32 16-bit words of a directory entry sum to zero. Mode word:
0100000 allocated, 04000 set-uid, 02000 set-gid, 0777 permissions.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success, 1 damaged archive or per-file errors, 2 usage or fatal error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
\*[PROG] -c -f src.tp -C project .            # magtape archive of ./project
\*[PROG] -c -D -f small.tp a.c b.c            # DECtape image
\*[PROG] -tv -f src.tp                        # long listing
\*[PROG] -x -f src.tp -C /tmp/out sub/dir     # extract one subtree
.fi
.SH BUGS
Only regular files are stored. Block padding is zero-filled; images written by
the original tp contain stale bytes there. Updating or deleting entries in an
existing archive is not supported; recreate it.
